+# Experimental iOS 16 port: source checkpoint 2
+
+This is an unfinished source port, not an iOS 16-compatible release or IPA.
+Target device for future testing: iPhone 13 Pro Max, iOS 16.0.
+
+## Implemented in this checkpoint
+
+- iOS 16 Swift package minimum declarations for the diagnostic compiler target.
+- Separate native-scroll library shelf for iOS 16/17, including search, favorites,
+  import, settings, selection, Play, game options, and controller selection.
+- Original iOS 18 carousel retained behind an availability boundary.
+- iOS 16 callback/navigation overloads, empty states, labeled buttons, animation
+  fallback, and a guard around the iOS 16.4 hosting-controller safe-area API.
+- Newer SwiftUI keyboard interception/focus effects guarded at iOS 17. On iOS 16
+  native UIKit keyboard behavior remains; custom SwiftUI arrow/Escape handlers
+  are not backported yet. Touch and controller paths still require device QA.
+- Manual compiler workflow with diagnostics retained even when compilation fails.
+- An independent native compiler job for 12 real translation units (JIT allocator,
+  Wine/display/input bridges, native pool, and DXMT's Unix Metal bridge/cache).
+- Separate compilation of the three original AIR helper shader sources under
+  Metal 3.0 / iOS 16.0. No production shader metadata is rewritten.
+
+## Run the compiler checkpoint
+
+In a fork containing the modified files, select Actions → iOS 16 source compile
+check → Run workflow. The default runner is `macos-26`; the input can select a
+custom runner. GitHub must allow Actions in the fork. Run it from the branch
+containing the patch; a newly added manual workflow may need to be on the fork's
+default branch before GitHub shows its Run workflow button.
+
+On a Mac with Xcode 26+ and XcodeGen, from the repository root:
+
+```sh
+bash ci/check-ios16-compile.sh
+```
+
+The target compiles the app Swift sources and the Madeira Swift adapters with
+an iOS 16.0 minimum. A header-only MadeiraNative module exposes the existing C
+declarations; it supplies no emulator implementation. Static archives deliberately
+do not establish runtime symbol resolution. No executable, IPA, runtime helper,
+code signing, or release is produced. The existing production workflow is not
+converted into an iOS 16 build by this checkpoint.
+
+The second job runs `bash ci/check-ios16-native.sh`. It compiles the original
+native sources to arm64 objects with iOS 16.0 as the minimum and makes unguarded
+availability warnings fatal. Each unit gets its own log; one compiler failure
+continues to the remaining units before the job fails. Successful objects have
+load-command and unresolved-import listings retained for inspection. It does not
+compile all FEX/Wine/LLVM/media dependencies and does not resolve runtime imports.
+
+Download both `Iridium-iOS16-compiler-diagnostics` and
+`Iridium-iOS16-native-diagnostics` after the run. A failure is useful:
+its compiler errors identify the next concrete changes. A successful run proves
+only source compilation, not that native dependencies link or that games run.
+
+## Remaining runtime work
+
+1. **DXMT shader output:** `testrepos/Madeira/research/dxmt/src/airconv/airconv_public.h`
+   exposes Metal 310/320, with no 300 mode. `dxbc_converter.cpp` writes Metal 3.1/3.2
+   AIR metadata; `metallib_writer.cpp` contains 3.1 library metadata.
+   `ci/prepare-native-runtime.sh` builds helper shaders with `-std=metal3.1`.
+   A genuine Metal 3.0 backend requires reviewing generated instructions, shader
+   features, library headers, and Metal host API availability. Relabeling metadata
+   alone would not establish compatibility. This backend is not modified here.
+2. **DirectX 12:** Apple's documented converter output requirements name iOS 17+;
+   earlier-OS output has feature limitations. Inspect the exact pinned converter
+   binary and its emitted shader requirements before considering an older OS.
+   Do not promise DirectX 12 support as part of the first iOS 16 test.
+3. **Native dependencies:** app targets, native LLVM/FEX, DXMT, media, ANGLE, and
+   Wine build scripts have iOS 17/18 minimums. Rebuild every linked dependency for
+   iOS 16, fix unavailable APIs, and audit Mach-O load commands and imported symbols.
+   A lower app Info.plist alone does not solve this.
+4. **JIT:** BuiltinJIT explicitly requires iOS 18; its source build also has a
+   newer minimum. For iOS 16, exclude that helper from the eventual package and
+   validate an appropriate existing JIT path and real executable-memory allocation
+   on the device. A jailbreak or entitlement alone does not prove allocation works.
+5. **Final link and device test:** rebuild the real MadeiraNative implementation,
+   package an unsigned app only after checks pass, then test startup, JIT, a small
+   graphics workload, input, audio, saves, and shutdown on iOS 16.0.
+
+## Evidence and limitations
+
+Local source/privacy checks and Python regression checks are separate from
+Apple SDK compilation. The supplied checkpoint was prepared on Linux without
+Xcode, an attached iPhone, or a dispatched GitHub Actions run. No claim is made
+that the Swift changes already compile, install, or execute on iOS 16.
+The handoff accompanying the patch records the exact local test results.
+
+References:
+- Apple Metal Shader Converter: https://developer.apple.com/metal/shader-converter/
+- Apple Metal language specification: https://developer.apple.com/metal/Metal-Shading-Language-Specification.pdf
+- GitHub runner labels: https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/runners/github-hosted-runners
